# ── Server ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
PORT=3000
NODE_ENV=production

# ── Authentication: Authentik (OIDC) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# Login is delegated to a self-hosted Authentik instance (stood up by the
# home-lab-utils repo: `./authentik.sh up`). Create an OAuth2/OpenID provider +
# application (slug `myfinancehub`) in Authentik, then set these. On the homelab
# box, put OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET (and the rest) in the host-only finance.env file
# that deploy.sh injects with --env-file — never commit the secret.
#
# Use the homelab HOST IP (not a container DNS name) for the issuer so the OIDC
# `iss` claim matches what the browser and the app server both reach.
# OIDC_ISSUER=http://<homelab-ip>:9000/application/o/myfinancehub/
# OIDC_CLIENT_ID=
# OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=
#
# ── Roles (Authentik groups → app role) ───────────────────────────────────────
# Three roles: admin > member > viewer (admin = everything, member = everyday
# writes, viewer = read-only). The role is taken from the user's Authentik group,
# delivered in the OIDC `groups` claim, and re-evaluated on every login.
# Authentik's DEFAULT `profile` scope mapping already emits `groups`, so no custom
# scope mapping is needed — just create the three groups and assign users.
# WARNING: once groups flow, a user in NONE of the groups becomes `viewer` on next
# login — add your admin user to finance-admins BEFORE enabling, or lose access.
# OIDC_SCOPE=openid profile email          # default; groups arrives via `profile`
# OIDC_ADMIN_GROUP=finance-admins
# OIDC_MEMBER_GROUP=finance-members
# OIDC_VIEWER_GROUP=finance-viewers
# OIDC_DEFAULT_ROLE=viewer                 # role when a user is in none of the groups
#
# OIDC_PUBLIC_BASE — set this only when Authentik is also exposed over Tailscale so
# login works OFF-LAN. It is the public Authentik ORIGIN (scheme+host[:port]) that
# the BROWSER can reach. For https (Tailscale) requests the app rewrites just the
# authorize URL's origin to this; discovery/token/jwks still use the LAN OIDC_ISSUER
# (which the container reaches directly), so the `iss` claim keeps validating.
#   tailscale serve --bg --https=8443 http://localhost:9200   # on the homelab
# OIDC_PUBLIC_BASE=https://<machine>.<tailnet>.ts.net:8443
#
# OIDC_REDIRECT_URI is now only a FALLBACK. The app derives the callback from the
# origin each request arrives on (honouring Tailscale's X-Forwarded-* headers), so
# it works on both the LAN URL and the Tailscale HTTPS URL without bouncing you to
# a single host. IMPORTANT: register EVERY origin's `/auth/callback` on the
# Authentik provider — easiest is a Redirect URIs *regex* covering both, e.g.
#   ^http://<homelab-ip>:3090/.*$
#   ^https://<machine>\.<tailnet>\.ts\.net/.*$
# OIDC_REDIRECT_URI=http://<homelab-ip>:3090/auth/callback
# Set to ANY value to enable RP-initiated logout (clears the Authentik SSO session
# too). The post-logout target is derived from the request origin, not this value.
# OIDC_POST_LOGOUT_REDIRECT_URI=1

# ── Session Secret (optional) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# If not set, one is auto-generated and stored in the database.
# Only set this if you need sessions to survive a full database reset.
# Generate with: node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# SESSION_SECRET=

# ── Monthly report email via Resend ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Required for the month-end report email. NEVER commit the real key.
# On the homelab box, put these in the host-only file /home/phanikumar/finance-hub/finance.env
# (chmod 600) — deploy.sh injects it into the container with --env-file.
# Get a key at https://resend.com/api-keys
# RESEND_API_KEY=re_xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx
# REPORT_EMAIL_FROM=onboarding@resend.dev
# REPORT_EMAIL_TO=maruthi.phanikumar@yahoo.com
# Optional: also mirror the report to Slack as a Block Kit card. Best-effort —
# a bad/missing webhook is logged and ignored, the email still sends. Host-managed
# in finance.env like the keys above. Create one at
# https://api.slack.com/messaging/webhooks (Incoming Webhooks). Unset = email-only.
# SLACK_WEBHOOK_URL=https://hooks.slack.com/services/xxx/yyy/zzz

# ── Natural-language query ("Ask") via local Ollama ───────────────────────────
# The "Ask" view turns a plain-English question into a READ-ONLY SQL SELECT using
# a local Ollama model — your finance data never leaves the homelab. Point these
# at the same Ollama box the home-lab-utils worker uses. Use the homelab HOST IP
# (not localhost / host.docker.internal) so the container can reach it, and make
# sure the chosen model is pulled (`ollama pull gemma3:4b`) and Ollama binds
# OLLAMA_HOST=0.0.0.0. The endpoint degrades gracefully if Ollama is unreachable.
# OLLAMA_BASE_URL — base URL of the Ollama server (no trailing /api/...).
OLLAMA_BASE_URL=http://192.168.50.34:11434
# OLLAMA_MODEL — model used for NL→SQL translation + summarising results.
OLLAMA_MODEL=gemma3:4b
# OLLAMA_TIMEOUT_MS — abort the model call after this many ms (default 30000).
# OLLAMA_TIMEOUT_MS=30000
# NLQUERY_ENABLED — set to 0/false/off to disable the Ask feature entirely.
# NLQUERY_ENABLED=1

# ── Temporal receipt extraction via local Ollama ──────────────────────────────
# The receipt-scan workflow extracts structured data from uploaded receipts with
# a local (vision-capable) Ollama model — separate from the "Ask" config above.
# LLM_URL — full Ollama /api/generate endpoint; host-managed in finance.env.
# LLM_URL=http://192.168.50.34:11434/api/generate
# LLM_MODEL — model used for receipt extraction. GitHub-managed (repo Variable
# LLM_MODEL); deploy.yml syncs it into finance.env. Make sure it's pulled.
LLM_MODEL=gemma3:4b
